      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Nature's Bounty protein powder is a single product: the Optimal Solutions Complete Protein and Vitamin Shake Mix, with 15 g of protein from soy and whey in a 33 g serving, 3 g of collagen and added vitamins. It carries no published third-party certification, its product page has gone from the brand's website, and stock is thinning. Below: the label line by line, cost per 25 g, buyer feedback and the alternatives worth buying instead.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Nature's Bounty Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nature's Bounty protein powder is a low-protein vitamin shake rather than a true protein powder, and most people will do better with something else. The Optimal Solutions Complete Protein and Vitamin Shake Mix gives 15 g of protein, 110 to 120 calories, 4 g of fiber, 3 g of collagen and a list of vitamins and minerals per two-scoop, 33 g serving, per the label filed with the NIH Dietary Supplement Label Database. At the drugstore and grocery prices we found, it costs about $2.50 to $3.14 per 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a light, vitamin-fortified breakfast shake and already like the taste. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you want 25 g or more of protein per scoop, a transparent protein source, third-party testing or a product you can rely on finding next month. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-women/">best protein powders for women</a> list covers stronger options built for the same shopper.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Per 33 g Serving (Label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein (soy + whey)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">110-120</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories by flavor</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Collagen</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2.50+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cost per 25 g protein</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Nature's Bounty Protein Powder and Better Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first two cards are the two Nature's Bounty shake mix flavors still sold through drugstores and grocery chains. Nature's Bounty does not run its own Amazon storefront for these, so they carry no direct Amazon link. The other three are alternatives with more protein per serving, a clearer label or a third-party certification, with the price we recorded in our November 2026 label checks where we had one.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-sells" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Protein Powder Does Nature's Bounty Sell Right Now?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nature's Bounty sells one protein powder line in the US: Optimal Solutions Complete Protein and Vitamin Shake Mix, in Decadent Chocolate and Vanilla Bean, in a 16 oz (453 g) canister of about 13 servings. That is the product people mean when they search for Nature's Bounty protein powder, and it is still listed by CVS, ShopRite, H-E-B, Kroger-family stores such as QFC, and delivery apps at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The line is shrinking, and we want to be plain about it. The shake mix's product pages on naturesbounty.com now redirect to the brand's general product catalog, which lists gummies, softgels and capsules but no protein powder. The Walmart vanilla listing showed as out of stock when we checked, and some resellers mark the chocolate as discontinued by the manufacturer. Nature's Bounty has not, as far as we can find, announced a discontinuation, so treat it as a product being wound down rather than officially dead.
          </p>

          <p>
            A third product, Complete Plant Protein and Vitamin Shake Mix in Decadent Chocolate, appears in 13 oz canisters at CVS and on Instacart, with 15 g of protein per serving per the CVS listing and a smaller 29 g serving. We could not confirm its ingredient list or protein source from any listing we could open, so we have not given it a card. If you find it in store, read the panel before assuming it is dairy- and soy-free.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nature's Bounty also sells collagen supplements, but in gummy and capsule form rather than as a protein powder. If you came here for collagen rather than protein, our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen protein powder guide</a> and the <a href="/collagen-vs-protein-powder/">collagen vs protein powder explainer</a> are the better starting points.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Who Makes Nature's Bounty Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nature's Bounty is owned by Nestlé Health Science, which bought it in 2021 along with Solgar, Puritan's Pride and the other core vitamin brands of The Bountiful Company. The label on the shake mix still names Nature's Bounty, Inc. of Bohemia, New York, the company's long-time headquarters, and says it is made in the USA with select ingredients from around the world.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sports nutrition brands that used to sit alongside Nature's Bounty did not go to Nestlé. Pure Protein, Body Fortress and MET-Rx stayed with The Bountiful Company's previous owner, KKR, according to coverage of the deal at the time. That matters because many shoppers assume Pure Protein is a Nature's Bounty product; it is a sister brand from the same era, now under different ownership, and its powders are built for gym use rather than as vitamin shakes. Our <a href="/pure-protein-powder-review/">Pure Protein review</a> and <a href="/body-fortress-protein-powder-review/">Body Fortress review</a> cover those.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nestlé's vitamin portfolio has grown around gummies, beauty supplements and targeted formulas, which helps explain why a 2010s-era shake mix is fading from the range. The Optimal Solutions name now mostly appears on hair, skin and nail gummies. None of that makes the shake mix unsafe; it does mean you should not expect reformulation, new flavors or better availability.
          </p>

          <p>
            The shake has also changed over the years. The 2012 label in the NIH database used a blend of pea protein isolate, whey concentrate and rice protein, with fructose and stevia. The 2019 label, which is the most recent one filed, swapped to soy protein isolate and whey concentrate and added 3 g of collagen. Tubs bought today should match the 2019 label, but check the panel if you have an older canister in the cupboard.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is in Nature's Bounty Complete Protein and Vitamin Shake Mix?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Each two-scoop, 33 g serving of the chocolate shake gives 110 calories, 15 g of protein, 3 g of fat, 11 g of carbohydrate, 4 g of fiber and 2 g of sugar, per the 2019 label in the NIH Dietary Supplement Label Database. The vanilla gives 120 calories, 15 g of protein, 2 g of fat, 13 g of carbohydrate, 4 g of fiber and 6 g of sugar. Both list 25 mg of cholesterol and 110 mg of sodium.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein comes from a proprietary protein blend of soy protein isolate and whey protein concentrate, so the label does not say how much of each you get. The panel also lists 3 g of collagen, without stating its source or type, and does not say whether that collagen is counted inside the 15 g of protein. Collagen lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, so a shake that leans on it does less for muscle than the protein number suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond the protein, the label adds 100% of the old daily values for the B vitamins and vitamin C, 20 mcg of vitamin D, 220 to 240 mg of calcium, 300 mcg of biotin, an 850 mg ProbioGuard probiotic blend of Lactobacillus acidophilus and B. bifidum, a 200 mg enzyme blend of bromelain and papain, and 888 mg of flaxseed. Nature's Bounty pitches the formula as meeting key nutritional needs of women, with structure-function claims for skin, bones, energy, hair and digestion.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other ingredients are cocoa (chocolate only), inulin, oat fiber, fructose, natural flavors, soy lecithin, maltodextrin, tricalcium phosphate and xanthan gum, with stevia leaf extract listed in the panel at 160 mg in chocolate and 108 mg in vanilla. Fructose sits second in the vanilla's other-ingredients list, which explains its 6 g of sugar. The label says it contains milk and soy, has no artificial colors or flavors, and is not intended for anyone under 18.
          </p>

          <p>
            The directions suggest mixing two scoops with 8 oz of water, or with skim milk to take protein above 20 g. That advice tells you most of what you need to know: on its own, this shake does not deliver the 20 to 30 g of protein most research uses for a post-workout or meal-replacement dose. People who are pregnant, nursing, taking medication or managing kidney disease should check with a doctor or registered dietitian first, as the label itself advises.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Nature's Bounty Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No independent certification is published for it. We found no listing for any Nature's Bounty protein powder in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice databases, and the label carries no USP, NSF or Informed seal. What the canister does say is Laboratory Tested and Guaranteed Quality, which are the brand's own claims about its in-house testing, not a third-party audit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some retailers repeat a line that Nature's Bounty vitamins and supplements are third-party tested. The brand does not identify the tester or publish certificates of analysis for this shake, so we cannot verify that claim for the protein mix. Treat it as a quality-control statement, which is meaningful for a large manufacturer, but not the same as a named certification that you can check by lot number.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most casual users this is not a deal-breaker. It matters more for three groups: athletes who are drug-tested, who need a sport certification; people worried about heavy metals in plant and blended proteins, who need published contaminant results; and anyone pregnant or buying for a teen, who should prefer a certified product. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder rankings</a> list powders that meet that bar, several of them at a lower cost per gram than this shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more trust point: the label carries the standard FDA disclaimer and, unusually, a notice that the shake should be used for weight reduction only as directed in the Optimal Solutions diet plan and not in diets under 400 calories a day without medical supervision. That plan no longer appears on the brand's site.
          </p>
        </div>

